// WHAT THIS PINS: the async `send` returns `false` on a closed channel, and
// returns it *immediately* -- the `closed` check sits before the
// `yield_now().await`, so a closed channel with room is answered without a
// suspension. Before this fixture no test called `send` on a closed channel
// at all: `channel_close_refuses_send` uses the synchronous `try_send`, and
// `channel_two_tasks_blocking`'s producer closes only after every one of its
// sends has already returned. So the difference between `try_send` (false
// when full OR closed) and `send` (false ONLY when closed) was documented in
// three records and pinned by none of them.
//
// Both halves are asserted, and the first is what makes the second
// attributable: the same `send`, on the same channel, returns true while the
// channel is open and false once it is closed. A regression that broke
// `send` outright would move the first line too, so a lone `false` cannot be
// mistaken for correct behaviour.
//
// TERMINATION, and one honest limit. On the shipped code this fixture is
// bounded: `send` on a closed channel returns without ever yielding, so
// there is nothing to spin on. That is a property of the shipped ordering,
// not of the fixture. Deleting `send`'s own `if self.ch.closed { return
// false }` makes this fixture HANG rather than fail -- `push` still refuses
// every value on a closed channel, so the retry loop spins with no
// suspension point that can ever succeed, and `main` is the only task. That
// is the same unpreemptable-livelock shape `channel_recv_suspends_only_to_
// retry`'s comment describes, and it is not fixable from inside a fixture:
// any test that drives a send into an unsatisfiable retry loop must hang,
// because nothing else can run to satisfy it. What this fixture does catch
// as an ordinary bounded diff is the mutation that makes `close` stop
// refusing (`push` losing its `closed` guard), which flips the second line
// from false to true. Measured both ways; see ADR 0017.
async fn main() {
    let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)
    let mut tx = ch.sender()
    // Open, with room: true, and no suspension is needed to say so.
    println("open=${tx.send(1).await}")
    tx.close()
    // Closed, still with room: false. `try_send` would also say false here,
    // but for the other reason, which is exactly why this line needs `send`.
    println("closed=${tx.send(2).await}")
}
